Keep CS_VALID when nothing can validate a page.

parse_machfile() validates the first pages of every image it loads, and on
this kernel all of them fail:

    CODE SIGNING: cs_validate_page: ... off 0x6a000 size 0x1000:
        actual [0x0 0x0 0x0 0x0 0x0] != expected [0x9f3bc4d7 ...]

The computed side is zeros because there is no corecrypto: the SHA that would
hash the page is a stub that writes nothing. Every page of every binary comes
out the same way, so the verdict distinguishes nothing -- it is not that
these images failed a check, it is that the check does not exist here.

The loader already knows not to act on it. cs_process_global_enforcement() is
false with cs_enforcement_disable=1, so the load is allowed to continue. But
one line ran regardless of that:

    if (cs_process_global_enforcement() || (result->csflags & (CS_HARD|CS_KILL|CS_ENFORCEMENT))) {
            ret = LOAD_FAILURE;
    }
    result->csflags &= ~CS_VALID;

and that bit is load-bearing far beyond what it looks like. In
exec_mach_imgact():

    if (load_result.csflags & CS_VALID) {
            imgp->ip_csflags |= load_result.csflags &
                (CS_VALID | CS_SIGNED | CS_DEV_CODE | ...);
    } else {
            imgp->ip_csflags &= ~CS_VALID;
    }

the whole handover is gated on it, so clearing CS_VALID also drops CS_SIGNED,
and p_csflags ends up zero. That is visible on the console as

    cs_invalid_page(0x100eb4000): p=1[launchd] final status 0x0

and it was read, wrongly, as meaning only that the page had been rejected.
What it actually meant was that the process had no code signing state at all.
csproc_get_blob() then refuses to look the blob up --

    if ((p->p_csflags & CS_SIGNED) == 0) return NULL;

-- csproc_get_platform_binary() answers 0, csops(CS_OPS_STATUS) reports a
process with no signature, and launchd kills the boot:

    unexpected exec of non-platform binary: /usr/sbin/syslogd

So the clearing moves inside the branch that already decided the failure
matters. Where enforcement applies, the load has just been failed anyway and
the bit is cleared exactly as before; where it does not, the process keeps
the signing state its blob gave it.

This is a concession to a missing dependency, not a position on code signing,
and it should not outlive it. Once corecrypto is built in the check means
something again, every page either validates or genuinely does not, and
leaving this in place would be a real hole. Delete the patch then.

Note that cs_invalid_page() still clears CS_VALID on the process at fault
time, and still will. That is correct and it is harmless here: it leaves
CS_SIGNED alone, which is the bit csproc_get_blob() actually asks for.
--- a/bsd/kern/mach_loader.c
+++ b/bsd/kern/mach_loader.c
@@ -1098,8 +1098,40 @@
 							if (cs_process_global_enforcement() ||
 							    (result->csflags & (CS_HARD | CS_KILL | CS_ENFORCEMENT))) {
 								ret = LOAD_FAILURE;
+								/*
+								 * CS_VALID is cleared only where it
+								 * still means something. This kernel
+								 * has no corecrypto, so the digest
+								 * that would hash the page returns
+								 * zeros and EVERY page fails here --
+								 * see cs_validate_page()'s
+								 * "actual [0x0 0x0 0x0 0x0 0x0]".
+								 * Clearing the bit on a verdict that
+								 * is the same for every binary tells
+								 * nothing apart, and it is not free:
+								 * exec_mach_imgact() gates the whole
+								 * flag handover on it,
+								 *
+								 *   if (load_result.csflags & CS_VALID)
+								 *           imgp->ip_csflags |= ... CS_SIGNED ...
+								 *
+								 * so one cleared bit also costs
+								 * CS_SIGNED, and without CS_SIGNED
+								 * csproc_get_blob() will not look at
+								 * the blob at all.
+								 *
+								 * Where enforcement does apply the
+								 * load has just been failed anyway,
+								 * so the bit is cleared as before.
+								 *
+								 * Delete this whole patch when
+								 * corecrypto is built in: the check
+								 * will mean something again, and
+								 * leaving it weakened would then be
+								 * a real hole.
+								 */
+								result->csflags &= ~CS_VALID;
 							}
-							result->csflags &= ~CS_VALID;
 						}
 						off += PAGE_SIZE;
 					}
